ily for five  years and a lot of this has been driven  by new types of drugs particularly spice  and I really believe that this can be  turned around but the way to turn this  around is to get back to basics it's  about making sure that we really improve  the perimeter security and that we  invest in supporting and training staff  we've got nearly four and a half  thousand more prison officers but it's  now making sure that they're trained  properly and supported properly and I  can already see in places like Leeds or  an Isis prison in London the beginnings  of green shoots the beginning of the  sense that we can turn this around but  it's a very very tough job it's a job  I'm I'm very proud to have I'm very  proud of the present officers and the  work they do but nobody should  underestimate how difficult it is partly  because violence in society is  increasing we began with knife crime  you'll hear about attacks even on on  ambulance workers it is a very troubling  moment and prisons are right at the  centre of that now you perhaps  understand the Middle East most more  than most politicians after walking for  thousands of miles through Afghanistan  Pakistan and Iran so I wanted to talk to  you a bit about the situation in Syria  just as we and the interview you  previously said this about British  jihadis fighting in Syria we have to be  serious about the fact these people are  a serious danger to us and unfortunately  the only way of dealing with them will  be in almost every case to kill them so  I'm interested to know what your  thoughts are on how we should deal with  the wives of Islamic state fighters  people like Shamima Begum how should we  deal with these people  Sophie I think the first thing to say is  that nobody should address them  how much of a threat these people  potentially can be I mean I've spent a  lot of my life working obviously in Iraq  and Afghanistan and people who join Isis  have a very varied assorted very cruel  very savage view of t